Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 102, "THE HIDDEN BLOODLINE"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 15 script pages of a 50-minute episode. It follows the Season 1 bible and the approved film. Canon this draft respects: the tin box holds a lock of hair, a child's drawing and a field-notebook page with nine rings, five numerals and the word Kharesh (film, Act One); Auren was nine when he met Seraphine and does not remember it (film, Act Two: "You met me when I was nine?"); Auren has only heard of Kharesh and never meets Lyra or sees Torin (Continuity Locks 3, 4, 6). One adjustment to the bible's beat sheet, so it can hold with the film: Seraphine meets the boy on a first visit to the bookshop that night, and Isolde leaves him with the neighbour before she returns alone. The film shows Auren reading this same page again in its present; here is the first time. The exact phrase "Hold the other end" is held back for episode 7 (Season 1 bible, recurring motifs); Ithren's and Isolde's vessel formula "For whoever holds the other end" is the episode 1 variant. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

EXT. ANVAR RIDGE — DAWN

A long bare ridge above a river valley, the grass flattened by wind. A dig in its second week: string grids, a generator coughing, three small tents. Cold, clean light.

TUAN PHAM, 30, geophysicist, kneels over a ground-penetrating array the size of a lawnmower, a headset around his neck and the camera, as always, on its strap. The array's screen shows a grey scroll of soil layers. He is half asleep.

HOLLIS REEVE, 50s, sits on an upturned crate with a thermos, doing the crossword in an old newspaper.

On the screen, the scroll of grey shows a single bright line, perfectly straight and perfectly steady, running through every layer like a wire.

TUAN
(not looking up)
Hollis.

HOLLIS
(not looking up)
Four letters. "Buried, in a way."

TUAN
Hollis. Come and look at my noise.

He lifts one headphone to his ear, and then, very slowly, takes both off and holds them out. A thin, steady tone leaks from the cups. Hollis puts one to his ear. His face does not change, which is how Tuan knows it is real.

HOLLIS
What frequency?

TUAN
(checking the readout)
Nine point one seven one seven hertz. To four places. It doesn't drift. A machine would drift.

HOLLIS
Is there a machine?

Tuan turns, slowly, and looks at the bare ridge.

TUAN
There's a lot of ridge.

He sets the array down. The line on the screen keeps running, as straight as a ruler, to the point of the slab they uncovered yesterday.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. AUREN'S FLAT, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

The chandler's shop below is shuttered. Upstairs, a single lamp. Auren sits at his table with the biscuit tin in front of him. He has taken it down. He has made tea he is not drinking.

The lid, scratched with a pin: a ring, a second ring inside it, seven small marks.

On the front of the tin, two brass catches a hand's span apart, one at each end. A child could open one. It takes both hands to open both, and that, he remembers, was the point: his mother, years ago, laughing, saying:

ISOLDE (V.O.)
(warm, from memory)
Two hands, Auren. One hand never gets you anywhere interesting.

Auren puts a thumb on each catch. Breathes in. Presses.

They open together with a soft double click.

Inside, a lock of dark hair tied with red thread. A child's drawing in wax crayon: a wobbling target of nine circles, with a small figure standing outside it, arms wide. At the bottom, in a boy's capitals, AUREN 9. And a folded sheet of paper, soft with age.

He opens the sheet. It is a page torn from a field notebook, written in a slanted hand he would know in the dark.

INSERT — THE PAGE. A sketch of nine concentric rings, seven filled, two empty. Below it, five numerals in a row:

9 1 7 1 7

And under them, a single word, underlined twice: KHARESH.

AUREN
(reading, a whisper)
"If you ever hear it, don't follow it. Let it pass."

He stops. Turns the paper over. On the back, in smaller letters:

AUREN (CONT'D)
"...but if you can't, go to the ones who dig."

He lays the page down very carefully, as though it might wake. He looks at the five numerals. He looks, without turning, at the dark window, where last night the same five digits were written in breath.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
That's not a coincidence.

He says it like a question. The coins on his shelf, in a bowl he never emptied, answer with a faint, single note.

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — DAY

Nell, at her bench, with a mug (the new one, a stubborn blue). Auren arrives early, hair wet, a tin under his arm that he does not put down.

AUREN
Is Kharesh a name?

NELL
(not looking up)
It's a... bit of a mouthful. Like a surname? A family you know?

AUREN
I don't know anyone called it.

NELL
(now looking)
Is this about your mum?

He does not answer, which is an answer. Nell puts her mug down.

NELL (CONT'D)
There's a Kharesh in the registry. A lender. Eighteen-nineties, a family of customs clerks.

AUREN
(grasping)
Customs clerks.

NELL
(carefully)
Auren. A word in a notebook is not a family.

AUREN
No. But she underlined it twice.

Nell is quiet. Then she turns to her screen and starts to type, because she is Nell.

NELL
Show me the hand.

ACT TWO

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH, PORT VESSA — NIGHT (FIFTEEN YEARS EARLIER)

Trains thunder overhead, the arch trembling dust from its beams. Inside, a long narrow bookshop in warm lamplight, shelves to the curved ceiling and a rolling ladder no one has used in years.

SERAPHINE stands behind the counter in a bookseller's cardigan, reading a ledger, exactly as she is in the present day. She has not aged a day, and in fifteen years no one has noticed, because no one in a bookshop looks at the bookseller.

The door opens on a bell. ISOLDE VALE, 30s, in a headscarf, a canvas bag over one shoulder, comes in with a boy of nine by the hand: AUREN, small for his age, serious, a cardigan with the sleeves too long.

ISOLDE
(bright, a little too bright)
We won't be a minute. He wants something to read on the walk home.

SERAPHINE
(gently)
Take as long as you like.

Isolde glances at the shelves, at the ladder, at the dark end of the shop, with the quick distracted eye of someone checking exits. The boy lets go of her hand and wanders. He stops before a tall case.

On its top shelf, well above any child, a single slim volume bound in dull green.

He looks at it. Looks round. Drags a wooden chair across the boards, climbs it, reaches. His fingertips are two inches short.

Seraphine glances up. She sees him. She goes completely still.

AUREN (9)
(to the book, to himself, with perfect logic)
If I can't get it down, who can?

Seraphine says nothing. Something crosses her face, long and slow, like the shadow of a very large bird. She puts down the ledger.

SERAPHINE
(very softly)
You can't reach it yet.

AUREN (9)
(not turning)
I will.

Isolde is suddenly beside him, lifting him from the chair with a laugh that is mostly breath.

ISOLDE
Thank you, we'll come back for it.

SERAPHINE
(holding the boy's eyes)
Yes. Do.

Isolde carries him out. The bell rings. The shop is very quiet. Seraphine takes the green book from the high shelf, with no ladder and no chair, only a long careful reach, and holds it a moment against her chest.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(to the empty shop)
Here it is.

EXT. A QUIET STREET ABOVE A FISHMONGER'S — NIGHT (MINUTES LATER)

Isolde, with the boy, at a green door. A neighbour opens: MRS ABARA, sixty, in a dressing gown, with the warm weary face of a woman who has said yes to many things.

ISOLDE
Just the night. I'll be back before he's awake.

MRS ABARA
(looking at Isolde, not the boy)
You said that last week.

ISOLDE
(an apology)
I know.

She crouches to the boy, holds his shoulders, and looks at him for much longer than a neighbour's doorstep deserves.

ISOLDE (CONT'D)
Two hands, Auren. Whatever happens. Never one.

AUREN (9)
Mum, you're squeezing.

She lets go. Smiles. Kisses his forehead. Does not look back.

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH — LATER THAT NIGHT

The bell of the door. Isolde, alone now, breathless, closes it behind her. Seraphine is in the back, up a short flight of stairs, with the lamp on, reading. She has no reason to come down.

Isolde moves quickly and quietly through the shop to the dark end, kneels at the lowest shelf and removes three volumes. Behind them, a panel in the wood. She slides it open. Inside, a hollow lined with sand.

From her bag she takes a small clay vessel sealed with wax and a thread of silver. She sets it in the hollow. She does not let go at once.

ISOLDE
(under her breath)
For whoever holds the other end.

The panel closes. She puts the three books back. She stands. She looks up the stairs, toward the lamplight, and a very small, very tired smile crosses her face, as though she had half expected to be seen.

She goes out. The bell rings.

Upstairs, Seraphine turns a page. She does not look up.

EXT. THE RAILWAY ARCH — MOMENTS LATER

Two black cars slide to the kerb. Two men in dark coats get out: REUNION OPERATIVES, unhurried, one carrying a small case with a brass lock. They try the shop door, which opens. They go in.

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH — CONTINUOUS

The two men move through the shop, looking at shelves. One stops at the lowest, runs a finger along the spines, and finds nothing.

Seraphine appears at the top of the stairs with a book in her hand and her glasses on her forehead.

SERAPHINE
We're closed.

OPERATIVE ONE
(courteous)
A woman came in. In a headscarf.

SERAPHINE
Several. It's a bookshop.

A pause. The two men look at each other, and then at the bookseller. She looks back with perfect, mild, bookish blankness. They leave. The bell rings. The trains thunder overhead.

Seraphine waits until the cars are gone. Then she goes down, kneels at the lowest shelf, and puts her palm flat against the wood where the panel is. She does not open it. She does not need to.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(softly)
Somebody else has begun.

ACT THREE

EXT. ANVAR RIDGE — DAY

The slab is cleared. Two metres by three, pale stone, the surface covered in fine shallow grooves: a lattice, nodes linked by threads, nine rings at its heart.

LYRA THORNE, 37, in the same field jacket, kneels on the edge of the slab with a brush and a notebook, utterly silent. Hollis and Tuan stand behind her. Tuan films, for once, with the camera actually switched on.

HOLLIS
(quietly)
Tell me it's not the same one.

LYRA
It's not the same one.

HOLLIS
Thank God.

LYRA
It's the same pattern. Different stone. Different century.

She lifts her brush and traces, in the air, the lines of a figure she has been drawing for twelve years: the nodes, the links, the nine rings, seven inscribed, two left blank.

LYRA (CONT'D)
That's the fifth continent. Hollis, that's five.

TUAN
(low)
The ridge hums.

LYRA
(not hearing, not yet)
It's not a map of a place. It's a map of how things connect. It's... a network.

She sits back on her heels. The wind pushes at her jacket. In the grey sky, an ordinary bird.

INT. A SMALL ARCHAEOLOGY PODCAST STUDIO (VIDEO CALL) — DAY

A screen within the shot: a young HOST in a bedroom studio with a ring light, and Lyra at her desk in Halden with the lattice pinned behind her. The word LIVE in a corner.

HOST
Dr Thorne, some listeners say you're chasing a myth.

LYRA
(smiling, tired)
They're welcome to. Myths are what's left when someone stops recording the facts. I'm trying to keep the record.

HOST
And the Anvar Ridge find?

LYRA
It's a network on stone. Four continents, five if this holds. Someone, thousands of years apart, kept drawing the same map, and nobody has asked what it's a map of.

INT. AUREN'S FLAT, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

Auren, at the table, laptop open, the tin beside it. The clip plays by chance: an autoplay he did not choose. The host's voice, then Lyra's.

LYRA (V.O.)
(from the laptop)
"A network on stone. Someone kept drawing the same map, and nobody has asked what it's a map of."

On the table, beside his mother's page with its sketch of nine rings, the lattice fills the screen behind Lyra. Nine rings at its heart. Seven filled. Two blank.

Auren's hands, flat on the table, begin to tremble.

The coins on the shelf hum. The lamp flickers. The tin box lies open on its side, the two brass catches glinting, a hand's span apart.

AUREN
(softly, to nobody)
Who are you?

On the laptop, Lyra's mouth moves. He is no longer listening to the words. He is looking at the pattern behind her, and under the pattern, under the street and the harbour and the sleeping city, a long way off, one clear note.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH — NIGHT

The present. The shop, a little dustier, a little smaller, the ladder still unused. Seraphine at the counter, the green book beside her, open at last at a page she has not turned in fifteen years.

On the shelf beside her, a small radio left on low. A thin voice speaks of "a network on stone."

Seraphine turns it up. She listens to every word. Then she takes off her glasses and closes her eyes.

SERAPHINE
(very low)
Someone has found the pattern.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 102 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
